{
  "_comment": [
    "Translates a legacy numbered block state from an imported build file into a",
    "block this game has. Read by src/schematic/mc_palette.cpp. The first number",
    "is the pre-flattening block id; the second is its 4-bit data value",
    "(orientation, species, colour), which is what most of the entries here exist",
    "to disambiguate.",
    "",
    "Row keys:",
    "  id          the legacy block id (required)",
    "  block       what EVERY data value of that id becomes (the row default)",
    "  variants    per-data-value overrides, written out or naming a set from",
    "              \"variant_sets\" below; a value mapped to null is skipped",
    "  family      the block a set's \"{family}\" placeholder expands to",
    "  skip        true when we have no block for it and it is dropped",
    "  substitute  true when the target is a stand-in, not the same block",
    "  fluid       true when the target is a liquid",
    "  still       the same substance with no fluid state, placed instead when a",
    "              paste is not asked for liquids (see \"fluids\" in the \"names\"",
    "              comment below). Without it a liquid cell is left out entirely",
    "  note        why, for whoever reads this next",
    "",
    "Policy, so the entries below are not arbitrary: a full opaque cube we do not",
    "have becomes a full cube (stone, cobblestone, snow) so the structure stays",
    "solid, while a partial or decorative shape (torch, sign, pane, carpet,",
    "trapdoor, flower) is skipped, because inventing geometry reads worse than a",
    "gap and the report lists every skipped state with its count. An id with no",
    "row is reported as unknown rather than guessed at.",
    "",
    "One id can need different targets per family and the data layout is the same",
    "for all of them, so the layouts live in \"variant_sets\" and each family is",
    "one line. If a pasted staircase climbs the wrong way, the set is the single",
    "place to fix it.",
    "",
    "The \"species\" and \"names\" sections below do the same job for the newer",
    "format, which stores states by name instead of by number; a file of either",
    "kind is translated by this one table, and a build only ever uses one of them."
  ],

  "variant_sets": {
    "_comment": [
      "The legacy data layout for a shape, shared by every material. Stairs: bits",
      "0-1 are east/west/south/north and bit 2 flips the stair over; data 2 is the",
      "side the full-height half faces. This game names a stair by the side its step",
      "rises toward and calls the northward one the plain block, so data 3 maps to",
      "the bare name."
    ],

    "stair_data": {
      "0": "{family}_e",
      "1": "{family}_w",
      "2": "{family}_s",
      "3": "{family}",
      "4": "{family}_e_up",
      "5": "{family}_w_up",
      "6": "{family}_s_up",
      "7": "{family}_n_up"
    },

    "_comment_slabs": [
      "Slabs: bit 3 is the top half. The stone slab's low bits are the material",
      "(0 stone, 3 cobblestone) and the wooden slab's are the species, so the two",
      "cannot share one set."
    ],
    "stone_slab_data": {
      "0": "{family}",
      "3": "cobblestone",
      "8": "{family}_top",
      "11": "cobblestone"
    },
    "double_stone_slab_data": {
      "0": "{family}",
      "3": "cobblestone"
    }
  },

  "species": {
    "_comment": [
      "Which of this game's four woods stands in for which of the other game's",
      "species. A \"names\" pattern captures the word in front of its *, and",
      "{wood} resolves that capture here. Whether a mapping is a stand-in is a",
      "fact about the mapping, so it is recorded on it: oak to oak is the same",
      "block, birch to our spruce is not. A captured word that is not listed",
      "makes the pattern not apply, which is exactly what stops a row like",
      "*_wood from swallowing stripped_oak_wood."
    ],

    "oak":      { "block": "oak" },
    "spruce":   { "block": "spruce" },
    "pine":     { "block": "pine" },
    "holly":    { "block": "holly" },
    "birch":    { "block": "spruce",  "substitute": true, "note": "birch reads as our spruce" },
    "jungle":   { "block": "spruce",  "substitute": true, "note": "jungle reads as our spruce" },
    "acacia":   { "block": "spruce",  "substitute": true, "note": "acacia reads as our spruce" },
    "bamboo":   { "block": "spruce",  "substitute": true, "note": "bamboo reads as our spruce" },
    "warped":   { "block": "spruce",  "substitute": true, "note": "warped reads as our spruce" },
    "crimson":  { "block": "pine",    "substitute": true, "note": "crimson reads as our pine" },
    "dark_oak": { "block": "pine",    "substitute": true, "note": "dark oak reads as our pine" },
    "cherry":   { "block": "holly",   "substitute": true, "note": "cherry reads as our holly" },
    "pale_oak": { "block": "holly",   "substitute": true, "note": "pale oak reads as our holly" },
    "mangrove": { "block": "holly",   "substitute": true, "note": "mangrove reads as our holly" }
  },

  "names": {
    "_comment": [
      "Translates a state that arrives *named* rather than numbered, which is how",
      "the newer build format stores everything: \"minecraft:oak_stairs\" with its",
      "properties. A key is a whole state name or a pattern with one *; an exact",
      "key always wins, and of two patterns the first that fits wins, so the",
      "specific rows stay above the general ones (the wood rows above the stone",
      "fallbacks, which are deliberately listed after them).",
      "",
      "The policy is the same as the numbered table below: a full opaque cube we",
      "do not have becomes a full cube so the building stays solid, a shape we do",
      "have becomes that shape in our version of the material, and something",
      "partial or decorative (torch, sign, pane, carpet, trapdoor, flower) is",
      "skipped, because inventing geometry reads worse than a gap and the report",
      "lists every skipped state with its count.",
      "",
      "Liquids carry \"still\" as well as \"block\": a paste places that one by",
      "default, so a build's water arrives as the still water the sea is made of",
      "rather than immediately running downhill, and /paste <file> fluids is what",
      "asks for the live source instead.",
      "",
      "A \"shape\" row is not a block: it names the family to use and reads the",
      "state's properties to pick the member, so one line covers all eight",
      "orientations of a stair. That is also the one place to look when a pasted",
      "staircase climbs the wrong way."
    ],

